data.the-service#The service:
bridge.admin.v1.AdminService (proto/bridge/admin/v1/admin.proto), with grpc.health.v1 beside it, which reports SERVING while the provider is ready.
Status version, issuer, start time; the federation’s metadata (IdPs, usable, valid until, last refresh, last error); what is held in memory RefreshMetadata fetch the federation’s metadata now, and say how it went ListIdPs the federation’s IdPs, as the institution list shows them (query, limit, 100 by default) ListClients the configured relying parties RevokePerson end their refresh token families, the access tokens those bought, their logins and device grants in progress and their application password, and revoke everything the certificate store holds for them: SSH and X.509 certificates (into the next KRL and CRL) and WireGuard keys DisablePerson, EnablePerson refuse somebody here, at login and at every token, whatever their institution says, and revoke what they hold; optionally until a time DisableIdP, EnableIdP the same for everybody one institution vouches for; a metadata refresh does not lift it ListDisabled who is disabled, why, by whom, since and until when With reflection = true, against a test provider:
$ grpcurl -plaintext -unix $PWD/run/admin.sock bridge.admin.v1.AdminService/Status { "version": "v0.23.0", "issuer": "http://127.0.0.1:28473", "started": "2026-10-11T16:50:39.955689Z", "federation": { "metadataUrl": "file:///tmp/authn/fed/metadata.xml", "idps": 1, "usable": 1, "validUntil": "2026-10-18T16:49:48Z", "lastRefresh": "2026-10-11T16:50:39.956938Z" }, "counts": {} }

data.a-document-is-refused#A document is refu:
when it is not signed by that certificate, or is signed with SHA-1, or its signature covers another element than the root;
when it has no validUntil, or is past it. This is RENATER’s own metadata as it was published on 29 September 2026, read on 10 October:
$ authn-bridge check --config v/renater-expired.hcl authn-bridge: the federation's metadata: metadata expired at 2026-10-08T07:30:02Z when its validUntil is more than 28 days away: a document valid for years is one a replay keeps alive for years;
when it is older than the one in use (an earlier validUntil). It is validly signed and not yet expired, so nothing else would refuse it, and it may be the one with a key since revoked, served by whoever stands on the path;
over plain http to anything but loopback. The signature would catch a change, but not a proxy that holds back the new document and keeps serving the old one.

data.a-server-the-client-may-not-have#A serve:
A resource that is not in the client’s audience is refused with invalid_target:
$ curl -s --netrc-file etc/portal.netrc http://127.0.0.1:28473/token \ -d grant_type=refresh_token -d refresh_token="$(jq -r .refresh_token tokens.json)" \ --data-urlencode resource=https://fs-nantes.example.org/ -w "
%{http_code}
" | jq . { "error": "invalid_target", "error_description": "resource "https://fs-nantes.example.org/" is not one this client may ask a token for" } 400 A refused refresh does not spend the refresh token. The request is checked before the token is used up, so the same refresh token still works. Asked for no resource, it gives a token for the client’s whole audience:
$ curl -s --netrc-file etc/portal.netrc http://127.0.0.1:28473/token \ -d grant_type=refresh_token -d refresh_token="$(jq -r .refresh_token tokens.json)" \ -o tokens.json -w "%{http_code}
" 200 $ jq -r .access_token tokens.json | cut -d. -f2 | tr "_-" "/+" | jq -R "@base64d | fromjson | {aud, client_id, scope}" { "aud": [ "https://fs-paris.example.org/", "https://fs-lyon.example.org/" ], "client_id": "portal", "scope": "openid profile" } The value is compared with the audience entries as a string, not normalised as a URI. The same server without its final / is a different resource:
$ curl -s --netrc-file etc/portal.netrc http://127.0.0.1:28473/token \ -d grant_type=refresh_token -d refresh_token="$(jq -r .refresh_token tokens.json)" \ --data-urlencode resource=https://fs-paris.example.org \ -w "
%{http_code}
" | jq . { "error": "invalid_target", "error_description": "resource "https://fs-paris.example.org" is not one this client may ask a token for" } 400

data.bounding-the-grant-at-the-authorization-:
resource can also be sent with the authorization request. It then bounds the grant: the code, and the refresh tokens that follow, are for that server, and a later token request can name it again but never another one. After a login whose authorization request carried resource=https://fs-paris.example.org/:
$ curl -s --netrc-file etc/portal.netrc http://127.0.0.1:28473/token \ -d grant_type=authorization_code -d code="$(cat code)" \ -d code_verifier="$(cat verifier)" -d redirect_uri=http://127.0.0.1:9/portal \ -o tokens.json -w "%{http_code}
" 200 $ jq -r .access_token tokens.json | cut -d. -f2 | tr "_-" "/+" | jq -R "@base64d | fromjson | {aud, client_id, scope}" { "aud": "https://fs-paris.example.org/", "client_id": "portal", "scope": "openid profile" } $ curl -s --netrc-file etc/portal.netrc http://127.0.0.1:28473/token \ -d grant_type=refresh_token -d refresh_token="$(jq -r .refresh_token tokens.json)" \ --data-urlencode resource=https://fs-lyon.example.org/ -w "
%{http_code}
" | jq . { "error": "invalid_target", "error_description": "resource "https://fs-lyon.example.org/" was not part of the authorization" } 400 The authorization request may carry several resource values: it is the one parameter that may be repeated there. Each is checked, a value given twice counts once, and the grant is for the servers named. After a login whose authorization request carried resource=https://fs-paris.example.org/ and resource=https://fs-lyon.example.org/, the code gives a token for both, and each refresh can ask for either:
$ curl -s --netrc-file etc/portal.netrc http://127.0.0.1:28473/token \ -d grant_type=authorization_code -d code="$(cat code)" \ -d code_verifier="$(cat verifier)" -d redirect_uri=http://127.0.0.1:9/portal \ -o tokens.json -w "%{http_code}
" 200 $ jq -r .access_token tokens.json | cut -d. -f2 | tr "_-" "/+" | jq -R "@base64d | fromjson | {aud, client_id, scope}" { "aud": [ "https://fs-paris.example.org/", "https://fs-lyon.example.org/" ], "client_id": "portal", "scope": "openid profile" } $ curl -s --netrc-file etc/portal.netrc http://127.0.0.1:28473/token \ -d grant_type=refresh_token -d refresh_token="$(jq -r .refresh_token tokens.json)" \ --data-urlencode resource=https://fs-paris.example.org/ \ -o tokens.json -w "%{http_code}
" 200 $ jq -r .access_token tokens.json | cut -d. -f2 | tr "_-" "/+" | jq -R "@base64d | fromjson | {aud, client_id, scope}" { "aud": "https://fs-paris.example.org/", "client_id": "portal", "scope": "openid profile" } $ curl -s --netrc-file etc/portal.netrc http://127.0.0.1:28473/token \ -d grant_type=refresh_token -d refresh_token="$(jq -r .refresh_token tokens.json)" \ --data-urlencode resource=https://fs-lyon.example.org/ \ -o tokens.json -w "%{http_code}
" 200 $ jq -r .access_token tokens.json | cut -d. -f2 | tr "_-" "/+" | jq -R "@base64d | fromjson | {aud, client_id, scope}" { "aud": "https://fs-lyon.example.org/", "client_id": "portal", "scope": "openid profile" } With a state block, the grant’s resources are stored with the refresh token, so a restart does not widen it.
A resource the client may not have is refused at the authorization endpoint too, as an error sent back to the client’s redirect URI. One such value among good ones refuses the whole request:
$ curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} %{redirect_url}
" -G http://127.0.0.1:28473/authorize \ -d response_type=code -d client_id=portal -d scope=openid -d state=s1 \ --data-urlencode redirect_uri=http://127.0.0.1:9/portal \ -d code_challenge=E9Melhoa2OwvFrEMTJguCHaoeK1t8URWbuGJSstw-cM -d code_challenge_method=S256 \ --data-urlencode resource=https://fs-paris.example.org/ \ --data-urlencode resource=https://fs-nantes.example.org/ 302 http://127.0.0.1:9/portal?error=invalid_target&error_description=resource+%22https%3A%2F%2Ffs-nantes.example.org%2F%22+is+not+one+this+client+may+ask+a+token+for&iss=http%3A%2F%2F127.0.0.1%3A28473&state=s1 At the token endpoint, one resource per request. A token is for one server, for the grant’s servers, or for the whole audience; it is never for a list the token request chooses. A second resource there is refused like any repeated parameter:
$ curl -s --netrc-file etc/portal.netrc http://127.0.0.1:28473/token \ -d grant_type=refresh_token -d refresh_token="$(jq -r .refresh_token tokens.json)" \ --data-urlencode resource=https://fs-paris.example.org/ \ --data-urlencode resource=https://fs-lyon.example.org/ -w "
%{http_code}
" | jq . { "error": "invalid_request", "error_description": "resource appears more than once" } 400

data.the-rules#The rules:
where resource is taken by the authorization endpoint, and by the token endpoint with the authorization_code and refresh_token grants how many at the authorization endpoint, several: each is checked, and one that is refused refuses the request. At the token endpoint, one: a second is invalid_request what may be named an absolute URI without a fragment that is, character for character, an entry of the client’s audience the token’s aud the resource named at the token endpoint; if none, the resources the authorization request named, in the order given; if neither, the client’s whole audience a refresh may name one of the resources the authorization request named or, if it named none, any URI of the client’s audience. The new refresh token keeps the whole grant refused invalid_target, sent back to the redirect URI at the authorization endpoint and returned as a 400 error by the token endpoint. A refused refresh does not spend the refresh token other grants resource with client_credentials, at the device authorization request or when polling for a device token is refused with invalid_target, not ignored: a client that asked for a token for one server must not receive one for another. A device token is always for the whole audience the provider’s own scopes a token with ssh, nfs, app_password, wireguard or wireguard_peers is for the provider alone, and only the issuer itself may be named The refusal of other grants, at the token endpoint and at the device authorization request:
$ curl -s --netrc-file etc/portal.netrc http://127.0.0.1:28473/token \ -d grant_type=client_credentials \ --data-urlencode resource=https://fs-paris.example.org/ -w "
%{http_code}
" | jq . { "error": "invalid_target", "error_description": "resource indicators are taken with the authorization_code and refresh_token grants" } 400 $ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:28473/device_authorization -d client_id=files-cli \ --data-urlencode resource=https://fs-paris.example.org/ -w "
%{http_code}
" | jq . { "error": "invalid_target", "error_description": "resource indicators are taken with the authorization_code and refresh_token grants" } 400

data.example#Example:
Discovery and the request, with one IdP described by hand:
package main import ( "fmt" "net/url" "github.com/go-authn/saml" ) func main() { // One IdP, as a federation's metadata would describe it. idp := &saml.IdP{ EntityID: "https://idp.univ-example.example/idp/shibboleth", SSO: "https://idp.univ-example.example/idp/profile/SAML2/Redirect/SSO", Scopes: []string{"univ-example.example"}, } md := &saml.Metadata{IdPs: map[string]*saml.IdP{idp.EntityID: idp}} sp := &saml.SP{ EntityID: "https://app.example.org/saml", ACS: "https://app.example.org/saml/acs", Federation: md, } // Send the person to the discovery service, then read back what it chose. d, _ := saml.DiscoveryURL("https://discovery.renater.fr/renater", sp.EntityID, "https://app.example.org/saml/chosen") fmt.Println(d) _, err := saml.Chosen(url.Values{"entityID": {"https://idp.attacker.example/idp"}}, md) fmt.Println(err) chosen, _ := saml.Chosen(url.Values{"entityID": {idp.EntityID}}, md) // The AuthnRequest, over HTTP-Redirect. Keep p, keyed by the RelayState, // and hand it to sp.Accept at the ACS. to, p, err := sp.Request(chosen, "s1", saml.Options{AuthnContext: []string{"https://refeds.org/profile/mfa"}}) if err != nil { panic(err) } u, _ := url.Parse(to) fmt.Println(u.Host, u.Query().Has("SAMLRequest"), u.Query().Get("RelayState"), p.IdP, p.AuthnContext) // RelayState is limited to 80 bytes, and refused beyond that. _, _, err = sp.Request(chosen, string(make([]byte, 81)), saml.Options{}) fmt.Println(err) } https://discovery.renater.fr/renater?entityID=https%3A%2F%2Fapp.example.org%2Fsaml&return=https%3A%2F%2Fapp.example.org%2Fsaml%2Fchosen&returnIDParam=entityID the chosen identity provider is not in the federation idp.univ-example.example true s1 https://idp.univ-example.example/idp/shibboleth [https://refeds.org/profile/mfa] RelayState is limited to 80 bytes In a service, the metadata comes from the federation and the response arrives at the ACS:
package main import ( "context" "crypto/rsa" "crypto/tls" "crypto/x509" "encoding/pem" "log" "net/http" "os" "github.com/go-authn/saml" ) func main() { ctx := context.Background() // The federation's metadata signing certificate, downloaded ONCE and // checked against the fingerprint the federation publishes. fed := &saml.Federation{ URL: "https://pub.federation.renater.fr/metadata/fer/idps.xml", Cert: readCert("/etc/app/metadata-signature-2026.pem"), } if err := fed.Refresh(ctx); err != nil { log.Fatal(err) } go fed.Run(ctx, func(err error) { log.Print(err) }) pair, err := tls.LoadX509KeyPair("/etc/app/saml.crt", "/etc/app/saml.key") if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) } sp := &saml.SP{ EntityID: "https://app.example.org/saml", ACS: "https://app.example.org/saml/acs", Key: pair.PrivateKey.(*rsa.PrivateKey), // assertions arrive encrypted to it Cert: pair.Leaf, Federation: fed, } http.HandleFunc("/saml/acs", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) { p := pendingFor(r.PostFormValue("RelayState")) // what Request returned, from the session a, err := sp.Accept(r.PostFormValue("SAMLResponse"), p) if err != nil { log.Print(err) // for the log, not for the browser http.Error(w, "refused", http.StatusForbidden) return } id, from := a.Subject() log.Printf("%s (%s) from %s", id, from, a.IdP.EntityID) }) log.Fatal(http.ListenAndServeTLS(":443", "/etc/app/tls.crt", "/etc/app/tls.key", nil)) } func readCert(path string) *x509.Certificate { b, err := os.ReadFile(path) if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) } blk, _ := pem.Decode(b) if blk == nil { log.Fatal("no PEM block in ", path) } c, err := x509.ParseCertificate(blk.Bytes) if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) } return c } // pendingFor is the caller's session store. func pendingFor(relayState string) saml.Pending { return saml.Pending{} }
